Current original Zero workflow (0.12)

Use the native tools on Linux or their .exe versions on Windows:
  zero-integrate PROJECT_DIRECTORY NEW_KERNEL.zero
  zero-build PROJECT_DIRECTORY NEW_OUTPUT_DIRECTORY NEW_KERNEL.zero
  zero-check PROJECT_DIRECTORY NEW_TEST_DIRECTORY
All destination paths must be new. The integrator preserves authored sources;
the builder checks its compiler fixed point and builds the original targets;
the verifier checks language behavior and host file operations in process.
See tools/integrate.ztext, tools/build.ztext and checks/README.ztext.
Read these documents with zero-text read FILE.

The notes below preserve earlier implementation and test history. Any old
shell/Python runner names, generated artifacts, JSON reports or commands are
historical references: they were removed from the active project and are not
current runnable instructions or current-release validation. Native host
checks do not replace BIOS/UEFI, persistence or physical-hardware testing.

Historical implementation notes

Original Zero dword port tests

python3 review-tests/network-ports/run.py [CANDIDATE_COMPILER] [OLD_COMPILER]

Defaults to build/zero and bootstrap/zero-0.7-seed. The optional old
compiler enables exact old-source output comparison. The VM tests need the
384KiB or448KiB kernel-slot ISO, local QEMU/SeaBIOS paths in run.py, and
no writable disks. They copy/patch the ISO privately and leave the original
unchanged. Test programs themselves are Zero; Python only orchestrates files,
processes and result comparisons.

The suite compiles all five hardware targets, checks hosted rejection and
reserved/malformed forms, and runs native PCI mechanism1 tests in BIOS64,
BIOS32 without LM/PAE and real mode. Complete results are written under
verification/network-ports. Generated private images stay under build and
are not distribution artifacts. No native VirtualBox success is claimed.

python3 review-tests/network-ports/capacity.py [CANDIDATE] [OLD_COMPILER]
checks the expanded458752-byte output limit, exact458753 rejection for ELF/raw
and the final EFI padding boundary. These boundary programs are never executed.

If an integrated OS source now uses in32/out32 or exceeds the old output
ceiling, its old-source parity entry is explicitly skipped; the original
pre-integration parity run remains recorded in the historical report.
